the xbox series x's one terabyte  internal ssd is really fast  and can load up some games in a matter  of seconds but with 802 gigabytes  actually free to use you can fill it up  quickly with the size of games today  and don't forget the series s only comes  with a 512 gigabyte drive  so what are some solutions for expanding  your storage and how well do they  perform  in comparison i've got some answers for  you i covered this topic a bit in my  full comprehensive xbox series x preview  but i think this is important to  highlight on its own  and i have a few new details this time  around here look the three drives i've  used  the one terabyte seagate ssd expansion  card  a samsung t5 external ssd and a western  digital passport  external hard drive i'll start with the  one terabyte expansion card built  specifically for the series x and series  s this is the drive that plugs directly  into the console you just insert it and  it's immediately ready to use  it interfaces with the xbox hardware and  takes full advantage of that velocity  architecture microsoft has been flexing  in terms of speeds it's technically the  best solution and works identically to  the internal ssd  there's virtually no difference in load  times in the games we tested  however it carries a hefty price tag at  220 bucks  before we get into usb drives an  important thing to note is that they  cannot play series x or s  specific games off of the drive that's  where this expansion card comes in  clutch  especially once we start seeing more  series x and s only games  usb external drives can store those  games but they would have to be  transferred to the internal  drive or expansion card in order to be  played at least transfer speeds are fast  all things considered  these conditions can also apply to  certain xbox one games that have  received series x optimizations  as was the case for me with gears 5 and  gears tactics  otherwise you can play xbox one and  previous gen backwards compatible games  straight off a usb drive using a usb 3.0  cable and they'll still use the quick  resume game swapping feature without a  hitch  so onto an external ssd as i mentioned  earlier i used the samsung t5  with the usb c to usb 3.1 cable  and in terms of moving data around and  loading games the results were very  impressive  the outer worlds which is a 45 gigabyte  game took about 2 minutes and 54 seconds  to  transfer onto the external ssd and 2  minutes and 5 seconds to transfer back  to the internal drive  when loading into the same save we used  for our previous tests it only took 13  seconds off the external ssd  which is very close to the internal  drive that took 11 seconds to load  for final fantasy 15 and 84 gigabyte  file it took 5 minutes and 50 seconds to  transfer onto the external ssd  and just 3 minutes and 56 seconds to  move back to the internal storage  and the load time results were great as  well just taking 15 seconds compared to  the 13 seconds of the internal drive  both final fantasy xv and the outer  worlds are xbox one games without series  x optimizations  so the results between the internal and  external ssds are due to the fact that  the games are simply working with the  raw speeds of those drives  there are other external ssds out there  but the samsung t5 in particular  usually goes for around 110 bucks for  500 gigabytes and 140 bucks for one  terabyte now moving on to a traditional  hard drive as i said earlier i use the  western digital passport with a usb  3.0 cable and things start to slow down  but it's not all that bad  to transfer the outer worlds from  internal storage onto the external hard  drive it took longer about 7 minutes and  35 seconds  and transferring back to the internal  drive took 7 minutes however in-game  load times remain decent  taking 26 seconds to load up the same  save we used previously with final  fantasy xv the transfer speeds took  longer as well  coming in at around 14 minutes and 24  seconds to move the game to the hard  drive and 12 minutes and 55 seconds to  get back onto internal storage  loading up save data remain reasonable  though taking 52 seconds for the same  save file we used before  it's no surprise that our hard drive is  going to be slower compared to an ssd  but these results aren't bad at all  and they're actually faster than current  gen consoles 2. the outer worlds took 43  seconds and final fantasy 15 took 1  minute and 11 seconds to load saves when  using the xbox one x  considering you get more space for  cheaper with a hard drive this would be  the cost effective route for bulk  storage or playing backwards compatible  games if you don't mind the slower load  times a western digital passport goes  for just 80 bucks for 2 terabytes and  110 bucks for a whopping 4 terabytes  so what's the best solution of course it  depends on what you want to get out of  your storage drives  for backwards compatible games an  external ssd would be ideal since you  still get quick load speeds  and transferring data is quite fast  which is ideal if you're frequently  moving series x specific games back and  forth  if cheaper mass storage is more of a  priority an external hard drive is the  way to go  load times for back and pack games are  still better than current gen systems  but you will miss out on those sweet ssd  speeds  transferring data takes considerably  longer but the importance between 6  minutes and 14 minutes to move an 85  gigabyte file well that's up to you  that one terabytes you get expansion  card sure is nice and 220 bucks  is fair if compared to other nvme ssds  for pc but that's still pretty steep  when looking at other solutions for the  series x  and s if you have any more questions  please drop them in the comments below  or hit me up on twitter at michaelp hyam  or if you want to see other games tested  let me know and don't forget to check  out my full xbox series x preview  and our weekly show gen next thanks for  watching and i'll